WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.084
Choose local models by hardware fit, task fit, memory, speed, quality, stability, privacy,

00:00:06.084 --> 00:00:07.465
and operational needs.

00:00:07.465 --> 00:00:12.068
No parameter count, benchmark, or model family is universally best.

00:00:12.068 --> 00:00:17.591
Inventory CPU, GPU, VRAM, unified memory, system RAM, storage, acceleration support, power,

00:00:17.591 --> 00:00:18.512
and cooling.

00:00:18.512 --> 00:00:22.194
Evaluate the complete model, quantization, runtime, context, offload,

00:00:22.194 --> 00:00:23.575
and concurrency configuration.

00:00:23.575 --> 00:00:28.638
Smaller models can be faster, stable, and excellent for focused work.

00:00:28.638 --> 00:00:33.701
Larger models may improve difficult tasks but increase memory, latency, power,

00:00:33.701 --> 00:00:35.082
and deployment complexity.

00:00:35.082 --> 00:00:37.844
Quantization and context reshape those tradeoffs.

00:00:37.844 --> 00:00:41.986
Benchmark representative prompts repeatedly and record quality, refusals, hallucinations,

00:00:41.986 --> 00:00:47.050
first-token delay, prompt speed, generation, memory, thermals, failures, and workflow success.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:53.033
Start with a smaller reliable model, then scale when measured shortcomings justify it.

00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:58.557
The best local model is the one that performs acceptably on the

00:00:58.557 --> 00:01:03.620
actual machine for the actual tasks with enough margin to operate.
